WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.602
Real object access extends beyond slash
objects slash ID.

2
00:00:03.682 --> 00:00:07.987
A route such as slash projects slash
project ID slash tasks slash task ID contains

3
00:00:08.067 --> 00:00:10.409
two references and a claimed relationship.

4
00:00:10.489 --> 00:00:14.351
Authorizing the project alone does not
prove that the task belongs to it.

5
00:00:14.431 --> 00:00:18.673
Authorizing the task alone may omit the
project membership or tenant boundary.

6
00:00:18.753 --> 00:00:22.425
A secure lookup constrains the task
identifier, its parent project,

7
00:00:22.505 --> 00:00:25.987
and the authorized tenant or membership
relationship together,

8
00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:29.485
or verifies those facts through an
equivalent explicit policy.

9
00:00:29.565 --> 00:00:32.858
Apply the same reasoning to attachments,
comments, exports,

10
00:00:32.938 --> 00:00:36.673
search results, filenames, email lookups,
slugs, and alternate IDs.

11
00:00:36.753 --> 00:00:40.931
A download endpoint can bypass the policy
enforced on the record's normal read

12
00:00:41.011 --> 00:00:41.391
route.

13
00:00:41.471 --> 00:00:45.839
An export may reveal many objects even
though its request contains only one job ID.

14
00:00:45.919 --> 00:00:49.275
Actions such as approve, publish,
transfer, resend, archive,

15
00:00:49.355 --> 00:00:53.533
and restore are object operations with
their own permissions and state rules.

16
00:00:53.613 --> 00:00:58.171
Inventorying every route that reaches a
resource is therefore part of authorization

17
00:00:58.251 --> 00:00:59.264
design and review.

18
00:00:59.344 --> 00:01:01.117
Bulk APIs multiply the decision.

19
00:01:01.197 --> 00:01:05.375
Authenticating a request and authorizing
one representative object does not

20
00:01:05.455 --> 00:01:06.848
authorize an array of IDs.

21
00:01:06.928 --> 00:01:11.486
Resolve every requested object inside the
trusted scope and ensure the resulting set

22
00:01:11.566 --> 00:01:13.465
corresponds to the intended input.

23
00:01:13.545 --> 00:01:15.761
Then apply action policy to every member.

24
00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:19.830
Define behavior for duplicates, missing
objects, unauthorized objects,

25
00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:20.922
and mixed tenants.

26
00:01:21.002 --> 00:01:25.561
Never silently process an unauthorized
subset merely because some IDs were allowed.

27
00:01:25.641 --> 00:01:28.236
Choose transactional semantics
deliberately.

28
00:01:28.316 --> 00:01:32.621
An all-or-nothing operation can reject the
batch before any side effect when one

29
00:01:32.701 --> 00:01:34.031
member is unauthorized.

30
00:01:34.111 --> 00:01:36.010
A partial-success API may be valid,

31
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.015
but it needs an explicit per-item result
model, isolation between items,

32
00:01:40.095 --> 00:01:43.134
and no leakage about objects outside the
caller's scope.

33
00:01:43.214 --> 00:01:45.620
For destructive or state-changing batches,

34
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:50.131
conditional database operations and
affected-row verification help prevent the

35
00:01:50.211 --> 00:01:52.870
authorized set from changing before
completion.

36
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.862
Nested and bulk endpoints are not special
exceptions;

37
00:01:55.942 --> 00:02:00.374
they apply the same subject-action-object
rule to relationships and collections

38
00:02:00.454 --> 00:02:02.100
rather than one simple record.
